3. Service-continuity contract

3.1 Contract tuple

For a service s, define

\boxed{\;\mathcal{K}_s = \bigl(A_{\min},\, L_{\max},\, P_{\max},\, T_{\max},\, W,\, q\bigr)\;}

with the parameter domains as in v0.1: A_{\min}\in[0,1], L_{\max} in ms, P_{\max}\in[0,1], T_{\max} in ms, W>0 in ms, q\in(0,1]. The tuple is service-specific; values are selected from the service's actual requirements, not assumed universal. The domains are part of the contract definition, not merely descriptive.

3.2 Effective service availability and the usability predicate

Let u_s(t)\in\{0,1\} be the service-usability indicator. Effective service availability over window W is

A_s(W) \;=\; \frac{1}{W}\int_{t_0}^{t_0+W} u_s(t)\,dt .

The predicate defining u_s(t) is specified per service profile. It must be defined at the application layer for the profile under study. For an interactive service it typically requires successful delivery of the required information units within the service-specific deadline; for an essential-messaging service it may require only successful delivery of the critical messages. Radio connectivity, RRC-connected state, or the existence of a transport path never by itself satisfies u_s(t).

3.3 Non-compensatory constraint logic

The contract imposes four constraints, and their satisfaction is a conjunction, not a weighted sum:

\boxed{\;
\mathcal{C}_s(t) \;=\;
\mathbb{1}\!\left[
\begin{aligned}
&A_s(W)\ge A_{\min}\;\land\\
&T_{\mathrm{outage},s}\le T_{\max}\;\land\\
&Q_q(L_s)\le L_{\max}\;\land\\
&P_{\mathrm{loss},s}(W)\le P_{\max}
\end{aligned}
\right]
\;}

Definition 3.1 (Non-compensatory compliance). A service state is contract-compliant at time t iff \mathcal{C}_s(t)=1. No constraint may be traded against another. In particular:

· Excellent latency quantiles do not offset an availability shortfall.
· Low average loss does not offset a single outage exceeding T_{\max}.
· A compliant Q_q(L_s) computed over delivered packets does not offset loss (§3.5, §4.3).

The "hard" label on A_{\min} and T_{\max} therefore means veto, not high weight. The formal relationship is conjunction; the Assurance Engine is not authorized to relax it.

Remark 3.1. A contract whose constraints are individually satisfiable but jointly infeasible for a given service profile is inadmissible. Contract admissibility is a design-time obligation: the values of \mathcal{K}_s must be selected so that at least one admissible operating state exists under the declared assumptions. Admissibility checking is a task for NCC-Bench v0.1, not a runtime function.

3.4 Hard constraints

A_s(W)\ge A_{\min},\qquad T_{\mathrm{outage},s}\le T_{\max}.

These are not interchangeable with latency percentiles or average performance.

3.5 Statistical constraints

Q_q(L_s)\le L_{\max},\qquad P_{\mathrm{loss},s}(W)\le P_{\max}.

The latency quantile population must be declared explicitly. Unless otherwise specified, Q_q(L_s) is computed over successfully delivered packets with valid end-to-end timestamps. Lost packets are reported separately and are not silently excluded from the compliance predicate: a system cannot achieve nominal compliance by losing its slow packets.

3.6 Reference service profiles

NCC-Bench v0.1 will instantiate two reference profiles as experimental design choices, not as canonical service categories. The purpose of declaring them here is to make the contract values and the usability predicate concrete enough to be reproducible, not to assert that they exhaust the service space.

1. Essential messaging — low rate, high reliability, tolerant of moderate latency but sensitive to loss and to outage duration. The usability predicate requires successful delivery of the critical message within a declared deadline.
2. Interactive / voice-like — latency- and outage-sensitive, tolerant of moderate steady-state loss but sensitive to continuous interruption. The usability predicate requires bidirectional exchange within a deadline short enough that the interaction remains usable.

These profiles are not sourced from the literature as canonical categories; they are chosen because they stress different parts of the contract and therefore exercise different parts of the assurance logic.

