4. Measurement semantics

4.1 Measurement layer

Every NCC evaluation must identify its measurement layer. The preferred point is the application layer when the claim concerns end-to-end service continuity. PDCP-level measurements may be used as a controlled abstraction when application-level modeling is unavailable, but must be labeled as protocol-layer measurements, not as complete application-service guarantees.

4.2 Transition definition

Definition 4.1 (Transition). Let the active communication path at time t be

p(t) = \bigl(\mathrm{RAT}(t),\ \mathrm{cell}(t),\ \mathrm{bearer}(t)\bigr),

where \mathrm{RAT}\in\{\mathrm{TN},\mathrm{NTN}\}, \mathrm{cell}(t) identifies the serving cell or satellite beam, and \mathrm{bearer}(t) identifies the active bearer configuration.

A transition is an interval [t_i, t_f] during which p(t_i^-)\neq p(t_f^+). The transition is initiated at t_i by a 3GPP procedure (handover, path switch, RAT change, or beam switch) and completed at t_f when the target path is usable or the attempt is abandoned.

The transition-scoped evaluation window is [t_i-\Delta_{\mathrm{pre}},\, t_f+\Delta_{\mathrm{post}}], with \Delta_{\mathrm{pre}},\Delta_{\mathrm{post}} declared in the evaluation protocol. The sliding window W of §3.2 is evaluated independently and is not replaced by the transition-scoped window.

4.3 Survivorship-bias prohibition

The following are prohibited in any NCC compliance report:

· excluding lost packets from the latency population without a corresponding loss report;
· reporting Q_q(L_s) over a delivered-packet population that differs from the population used for the loss constraint;
· treating a reordered or duplicated packet as both delivered and lost.

In-flight packets are assigned to the transition interval in which they were transmitted and are accounted for exactly once, under a declared rule frozen in NCC-Bench v0.1.

4.4 False-authorization ground truth

Definition 4.2 (Ground-truth false authorization). For a transition instance i, let y^*(i)\in\{\textsc{permit},\textsc{non-permit}\} be the ground-truth outcome of the proposed action, where \textsc{non-permit} means the contract was violated in the realized transition. Let \mathrm{decision}(i)\in\{\textsc{auth},\textsc{rej},\textsc{unc}\} be the Assurance Engine's output. The false-permit rate is

\mathrm{FPR} \;=\; \frac{\bigl|\{\,i : y^*(i)=\textsc{non-permit}\ \land\ \mathrm{decision}(i)=\textsc{auth}\,\}\bigr|}{\bigl|\{\,i : y^*(i)=\textsc{non-permit}\,\}\bigr|}.

y^*(i) is observable only in simulation. In deployment, \mathrm{FPR} is not directly estimable; a measured-violation rate may be reported with a stated measurement model, but it must be labeled as an estimate, not as ground-truth FPR.

4.5 Per-packet deadline reporting

When the service profile defines a hard per-packet deadline, individual packet-level deadline violations must be reported separately, alongside the quantile and loss constraints. A compliant Q_q(L_s) does not establish that every packet met its deadline, and a per-packet deadline violation is not offset by a compliant quantile.

