5. Architecture and operational sequence

5.1 Stage 1 — Observe

Collect connectivity and signal state, latency and loss measurements, mobility state, available resources, predicted connectivity evolution, prediction uncertainty, and information age. Information age and uncertainty are first-class inputs, not descriptive metadata.

5.2 Stage 2 — Propose

The AI Decision Engine, or another controller, proposes a transition, a maintenance decision, or another supported mobility action. The decision engine is not assumed infallible.

5.3 Stage 3 — Verify

The Assurance Engine evaluates the proposal against \mathcal{K}_s and the current operating assumptions. It must not treat a prediction as a verified fact. Its evaluation consumes certified evidence (§2.3, §8), not raw point predictions.

5.4 Stage 4 — Authorize, reject, or declare uncertain

Let \theta denote a generic contract quantity, \theta\in\{A_s,\,T_{\mathrm{outage},s},\,Q_q(L_s),\,P_{\mathrm{loss},s}\}. Let

I_\theta(t;\delta) \;=\; [\,\theta_{\mathrm{lo}}(t),\ \theta_{\mathrm{hi}}(t)\,]

be a certified interval for \theta with coverage statement

\Pr\!\bigl[\theta \in I_\theta(t;\delta)\bigr] \;\ge\; 1-\delta ,

under a declared coverage assumption (§8.2). For a lower-bound constraint \theta\ge\theta_{\min}:

· conservatively satisfied iff \theta_{\mathrm{lo}}\ge\theta_{\min};
· certainly violated iff \theta_{\mathrm{hi}}<\theta_{\min};
· indeterminate otherwise.

For an upper-bound constraint \theta\le\theta_{\max}, the inequalities are reversed.

Definition 5.1 (Assurance decision).

\mathrm{decision}(t)=
\begin{cases}
\textsc{auth} & \text{if every constraint in } \mathcal{C}_s \text{ is conservatively satisfied},\\[2pt]
\textsc{rej} & \text{if at least one constraint is certainly violated},\\[2pt]
\textsc{unc} & \text{otherwise (at least one constraint is indeterminate).}
\end{cases}

The mapping from \textsc{unc} to a policy action is a declared policy parameter, not a theorem. Two mappings are specified and evaluated:

· Reference policy (fail-closed): \textsc{unc}\mapsto\textsc{rej}. Adopted as the primary reference because an unknown mandatory gate should not authorize action in a safety-relevant assurance layer. This is the default for primary results.
· Declared alternative (permissive): \textsc{unc}\mapsto\textsc{auth}_{\mathrm{restricted}}, where the restricted authorization class has a bounded validity period and mandatory re-verification before the transition executes. Evaluated as a sensitivity analysis, not as the primary policy.

The reference policy is not asserted as the only defensible choice; the permissive alternative is a legitimate policy for service profiles whose continuity requirement is non-safety-critical. The contract semantics are identical under both; only the mapping of the indeterminate outcome differs.

5.5 Stage 5 — Fallback

If the proposal is rejected, or if conditions deteriorate after authorization, a deterministic fallback policy is invoked. The fallback state machine is specified in §7. NCC does not assume fallback always succeeds.

5.6 Stage 6 — Measure

Post-action, NCC records contract compliance, hard- and statistical-constraint violations, per-packet deadline violations where the profile defines them, interruption and recovery times, fallback activation, emergency overrides, and energy and signaling overhead. The observed outcome is retained even when it contradicts the Assurance Engine's prediction.

5.7 Verification trigger model

Verification is not a one-shot event. The trigger model is

\mathcal{T} = \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{trans}} \cup \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{per}} \cup \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{evt}},

with:

· \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{trans}}: transition-scoped, fired at proposal (§4.2);
· \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{per}}: periodic revalidation every \tau_{\mathrm{reval}} ms;
· \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{evt}}: event-driven, fired on a material change (signal drop below a declared threshold, latency spike, loss burst, or uncertainty increase above a declared bound).

The parameters \tau_{\mathrm{reval}} and the material-change thresholds are declared in the assurance policy and frozen in NCC-Bench v0.1.

5.8 Authorization validity

An \textsc{auth} decision is valid for a bounded interval

\tau_{\mathrm{auth}} \;\le\; \tau_{\mathrm{reval}} .

At expiry of \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}, the authorization decays to the re-verification state; it does not persist by default. This prevents stale authorization from masking a deteriorating condition. The value of \tau_{\mathrm{auth}} must be shorter than the shortest TN–NTN reconfiguration timescale in scope.

