6. Single-path compatibility as a design constraint

NCC is designed under the realistic baseline assumption of a single active radio path. This is not a side note; it is a design constraint that shapes the assurance logic.

The constraint is motivated by the operating regime, not by an architectural preference. In the relevant 3GPP releases at the time of writing, DAPS is not supported for NTN, and a UE is not required to maintain simultaneous TN and NTN connectivity during a handover. Single connectivity remains the conventional baseline: a UE is typically served by one satellite gNodeB at a time in satellite-only operation, and LEO interruption times during handover fall in the tens-to-hundreds of milliseconds range even in releases that introduce mobility enhancements.

The consequence for the assurance layer is direct:

\text{NCC assurance}\;\not\Rightarrow\;\text{simultaneous multi-connectivity}

\text{NCC applicability}\;\not\Rightarrow\;\text{DAPS support}

The assurance process therefore cannot depend on make-before-break behavior, DAPS, or dual connectivity being universally available. Multi-connectivity, when supported, is treated as an additional operational resource; it is never a precondition for the contract's applicability or for the correctness of its measurement semantics.

