7. Fallback and emergency as a state machine

7.1 State set

S = \{\,\mathrm{MON},\ \mathrm{PROP},\ \mathrm{VER},\ \mathrm{AUTH},\ \mathrm{TRANS},\ \mathrm{FB},\ \mathrm{HOLD},\ \mathrm{EMERG},\ \mathrm{REC}\,\}.

State Meaning
\mathrm{MON} Monitoring; current path compliant.
\mathrm{PROP} Proposal received from decision engine.
\mathrm{VER} Assurance Engine evaluating.
\mathrm{AUTH} Authorization issued, validity \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}.
\mathrm{TRANS} Transition in progress.
\mathrm{FB} Ordinary fallback active.
\mathrm{HOLD} Safe-hold: degraded mode, no authority granted.
\mathrm{EMERG} Emergency override active; derogation logged.
\mathrm{REC} Post-transition measurement.

7.2 Transition guards

Let g denote a guard predicate over the current certified evidence.

From To Guard
\mathrm{MON} \mathrm{PROP} proposal received
\mathrm{PROP} \mathrm{VER} verification triggered
\mathrm{VER} \mathrm{AUTH} \mathrm{decision}=\textsc{auth}
\mathrm{VER} \mathrm{FB} \mathrm{decision}=\textsc{rej} and fallback feasible
\mathrm{VER} \mathrm{HOLD} \mathrm{decision}\in\{\textsc{rej},\textsc{unc}\} and no fallback feasible
\mathrm{AUTH} \mathrm{TRANS} transition initiated before \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}
\mathrm{AUTH} \mathrm{VER} \tau_{\mathrm{auth}} expired
\mathrm{TRANS} \mathrm{REC} target path usable
\mathrm{TRANS} \mathrm{FB} transition failed and fallback feasible
\mathrm{TRANS} \mathrm{HOLD} transition failed and no fallback feasible
\mathrm{FB} \mathrm{REC} fallback path usable
\mathrm{FB} \mathrm{HOLD} fallback failed, or fallback cannot satisfy \mathcal{K}_s
\mathrm{HOLD} \mathrm{EMERG} emergency condition declared (§7.4)
\mathrm{HOLD} \mathrm{REC} a compliant path becomes available
\mathrm{EMERG} \mathrm{REC} a compliant path becomes available; derogation closed
\mathrm{REC} \mathrm{MON} measurement complete

7.3 Safe-hold

\mathrm{HOLD} is the state entered when neither the proposed transition nor the current path satisfies the contract, and no fallback path is available. It is never a grant of authority. Service in \mathrm{HOLD} is not counted as compliant; it is reported as an outage under the T_{\mathrm{outage},s} constraint. This makes the boundary between assured operation and best-effort degradation explicit.

7.4 Emergency as constrained pass-through

The emergency override does not bypass the Assurance Engine. It passes through a constrained variant with:

· a declared emergency scope (affected service and path);
· a short-lived authorization with its own validity \tau_{\mathrm{emerg}}\ll\tau_{\mathrm{auth}};
· mandatory post-hoc replay of the evidence used;
· a derogation record opened at entry and closed at exit.

The override therefore preserves the evidence chain and identity constraints required for accountability, rather than removing them.

7.5 Derogation accounting

An emergency override is a logged contract derogation, not a compliant success. It is reported separately, with:

· emergency override frequency;
· interruption duration and recovery time;
· resulting hard and statistical violations;
· service degradation during the emergency;
· the reason ordinary assurance could not be maintained;
· the emergency scope and the \tau_{\mathrm{emerg}} actually used.

