8. Conditional assurance boundary

8.1 Certified compliance predicate

Combining §3.3 and §5.4, the certified compliance predicate at coverage 1-\delta is

\boxed{\;
\mathcal{C}_s^{\mathrm{cert}}(t;\delta) \;=\;
\mathbb{1}\!\left[
\begin{aligned}
&A_{s,\mathrm{lo}}(t)\ge A_{\min}\;\land\\
&T_{\mathrm{outage},s,\mathrm{hi}}(t)\le T_{\max}\;\land\\
&Q_{q,\mathrm{hi}}(L_s)(t)\le L_{\max}\;\land\\
&P_{\mathrm{loss},s,\mathrm{hi}}(t)\le P_{\max}
\end{aligned}
\right]
\;}

Authorization requires \mathcal{C}_s^{\mathrm{cert}}(t;\delta)=1. The interval endpoints are produced by the declared bound-generation method (§8.2).

8.2 Coverage assumption

The certified interval I_\theta(t;\delta) is valid only under a named coverage assumption. The assurance policy must declare one of:

1. Model-based bound. A parametric model with stated residual assumptions; coverage holds under the model.
2. Distribution-free conformal bound. Coverage holds under exchangeability of calibration and test residuals, with age-weighting to relax exchangeability under drift.
3. Worst-case deterministic bound. A declared uncertainty set; coverage is replaced by a guarantee over that set.

The method is a policy parameter; the reference implementation for NCC-Bench v0.1 is the age-weighted conformal bound, because it degrades visibly rather than silently under staleness.

8.3 Staleness

Let \tau_{\mathrm{age}} be the age of the evidence on which I_\theta is built. The certified interval must widen monotonically with \tau_{\mathrm{age}}:

\tau_{\mathrm{age}}^{(1)}\le\tau_{\mathrm{age}}^{(2)} \;\Longrightarrow\; I_\theta^{(1)}\subseteq I_\theta^{(2)} .

If the required width for coverage cannot be sustained at the observed \tau_{\mathrm{age}}, the engine returns \textsc{unc}, not \textsc{auth}. Certification is therefore a rate condition, not a state: it is sustainable only while the evidence-refresh rate exceeds the drift rate.

8.4 Failure conditions

If the coverage assumption cannot be established, or if the interval cannot be sustained at the observed staleness, the Assurance Engine must not classify the proposal as assured. The outcome is \textsc{unc} or \textsc{rej} under §5.4.

8.5 Trust boundary

The Assurance Engine is assumed to run in a separate trust domain from the decision engine. The security of that boundary — input integrity, policy integrity, and the inability of the decision engine to influence the evidence the Assurance Engine consumes — is assumed in v0.3 and out of scope for the formal semantics. This assumption must be discharged before deployment claims are made.

