10. Evaluation boundary

10.1 Architectures

NCC-Bench v0.1 compares three primary arms and two ablations:

Arm Decision engine Assurance Fallback
Baseline 3GPP mobility, no AI — —
AI-only D — —
NCC D \mathcal{A} \mathcal{F}
Ablation AI+A D \mathcal{A} —
Ablation AI+F D — \mathcal{F}

10.2 Decision-engine control

The AI-only and NCC arms use the same decision engine D: same model, same training data, same inference procedure. The only difference is the presence of the assurance layer and fallback. The difference in performance is therefore attributable to the assurance layer, not to a different underlying model. The ablations AI+A and AI+F isolate the contributions of assurance and fallback respectively. Any result reported without this control is not interpretable as evidence about the assurance layer.

10.3 Metrics, reported separately

Effective service availability; total interruption time; maximum continuous outage; hard-constraint violation rate; statistical-constraint violation rate; per-packet deadline violation rate where defined by the profile; packet-loss ratio; P95 and P99 latency; recovery time; false-authorization rate (§10.4); emergency override frequency and consequences; energy and signaling overhead.

A composite objective may be used for ranking but cannot replace the individual reporting of hard constraints (§3.3).

10.4 False-authorization reporting

\mathrm{FPR} is reported with the ground-truth definition of §4.4 and a one-sided 95\,\% Clopper–Pearson upper bound:

\mathrm{FPR}_{\mathrm{ub}} \;=\; \mathrm{Beta}^{-1}\!\bigl(0.95;\ k+1,\ n-k\bigr),

where k is the number of false authorizations and n the number of non-permit cases. For k=0, the zero-event bound 1-0.05^{1/n} is reported. FPR is reported only for the simulation track. Deployment-track violation rates are reported as estimates with a stated measurement model, not as ground-truth FPR.

10.5 Scope boundaries

The following are explicitly out of scope for v0.3:

· Multi-service arbitration. The contract is per-service. When multiple services share a path, the Assurance Engine evaluates each service independently and reports per-service compliance. A precedence order among services, if needed, is a design-time input, not a runtime function of NCC. The slice-assurance conflict-resolution pattern is the extension path.
· Assurance Engine trust boundary. Assumed, not discharged (§8.5).
· Deployment readiness. No deployment claim is made.

---

11. Open parameters for NCC-Bench v0.1

Service-specific usability predicates; application-level or PDCP-level measurement configuration; sampling and aggregation for A_s(W); in-flight packet accounting; latency quantile population and timestamp validation; sliding-window and transition-scoped evaluation; service-specific contract values and the admissibility check of Remark 3.1; \tau_{\mathrm{reval}}, \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}, \tau_{\mathrm{emerg}}; material-change thresholds; the coverage parameter \delta and the bound-generation method; emergency-policy state transitions and scope declaration; simulator support and abstraction limits.

These are operational specification tasks. They do not reopen the frozen contract structure of §3.

---

12. Research standing and next steps

NCC v0.3 establishes a formal baseline with uncertainty-bounded assurance and the validated positioning refinements of the v0.2 review. It does not yet establish:

· a mathematical theorem;
· universal service-continuity guarantees;
· superiority over Baseline or AI-only architectures;
· a demonstrated novelty claim;
· deployment readiness.

The next artifacts are:

1. NCC-Bench v0.1 — Metrics and Experimental Protocol, including the frozen trigger model, the admissibility check, the FPR reporting protocol, the two reference service profiles of §3.6 declared as design choices, and the ablation design of §10.
2. Related Work and Novelty Boundary — Adversarial Comparison, resolving the comparison with CAIF and EVIDENCENET and the broader family.
3. NCC Mathematical Model — Conditional Assurance Conditions, formalizing the certified-interval construction of §8 under each of the three coverage assumptions and proving the staleness-monotonicity property of §8.3 for the chosen method.

The central methodological rule remains:

No guarantee without explicit assumptions; no performance claim without reproducible measurements; no novelty claim without adversarial comparison; no authorization without a certified bound.

