Appendix B — Elementary consequences of the definitions

The four results below follow directly from the definitions of §3.3, §5.4, §7, and §8. They are stated because they are the properties the Assurance Engine is required to have, and because each is falsifiable by a single counterexample in NCC-Bench. They are not the conditional-assurance theorem of §12.

B.1 Veto lemma (non-compensation is preserved by the decision rule)

Lemma B.1. For any certified evidence state at time t, if any constraint in \mathcal{C}_s is certainly violated, then \mathrm{decision}(t)\neq\textsc{auth}.

Proof. By Definition 5.1, \textsc{auth} requires every constraint to be conservatively satisfied. If some constraint is certainly violated, it is not conservatively satisfied, so the first case does not apply; the decision is \textsc{rej}. \square

Observation. Lemma B.1 is what makes the word "hard" operational in §3.3. If the decision rule permitted weighting, a large surplus on one constraint could offset a shortfall on another, and the veto reading would be lost. Any implementation that computes a score and thresholds it violates Lemma B.1 and is therefore not an NCC implementation.

B.2 Certified authorization implies compliance with bounded probability

Lemma B.2. Suppose each of the four certified intervals is individually valid at level 1-\delta', i.e. \Pr[\theta\in I_\theta]\ge 1-\delta' for each \theta\in\{A_s,T_{\mathrm{outage},s},Q_q(L_s),P_{\mathrm{loss},s}\}. Then

\Pr\bigl[\,\mathcal{C}_s(t)=1 \,\bigm|\, \mathrm{decision}(t)=\textsc{auth}\,\bigr]\;\ge\;1-4\delta' .

Proof. Authorization entails, by Definition 5.1, that the interval endpoints satisfy the four inequalities of \mathcal{C}_s^{\mathrm{cert}}. The event \{\mathcal{C}_s=0\} is contained in the union of the four events \{\theta\notin I_\theta\}, whose probability is at most 4\delta' by the union bound. \square

Corollary B.2.1 (Bonferroni calibration). Setting \delta'=\delta/4 yields coverage 1-\delta for the joint predicate. This is the calibration used by default in §8.1.

Remark B.1. Lemma B.2 bounds the probability that an authorized action is followed by a realized contract violation under the coverage assumption. It does not bound \mathrm{FPR}, which is a frequency over realized transition instances (§4.4) and depends additionally on the decision engine's proposal distribution. The two quantities are related but not equal, and NCC-Bench reports both.

Remark B.2. The bound is conservative and ignores dependence between the four interval constructions. A sharper bound requires a joint construction — e.g. a single conformal procedure over the four-dimensional nonconformity score — which is a task for the Mathematical Model artifact (pending).

B.3 Authorization decay

Lemma B.3. If a transition is initiated at time t_e with t_e-t_a>\tau_{\mathrm{auth}}, where t_a is the instant of the last authorization, then \mathrm{decision}(t_a)=\textsc{auth} does not authorize the transition.

Proof. By §5.8, an authorization is valid on [t_a,\,t_a+\tau_{\mathrm{auth}}]. Transition initiation outside that interval requires re-verification; the state machine of §7.2 routes \mathrm{AUTH}\to\mathrm{VER} on expiry of \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}. \square

Design consequence. With \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}\le\tau_{\mathrm{reval}} and \tau_{\mathrm{auth}} shorter than the shortest TN–NTN reconfiguration timescale in scope (§5.8), a stale authorization cannot silently authorize a transition that executes after the evidence has materially decayed. The choice of \tau_{\mathrm{auth}} is the single parameter that converts the staleness requirement of §8.3 from an aspiration into an enforced property.

B.4 Safe-hold grants no authority

Lemma B.4. For every time t with q(t)=\mathrm{HOLD}, no service time in \mathrm{HOLD} is credited to A_s(W), and no authorization is in force.

Proof. By §7.3, \mathrm{HOLD} is entered only when no compliant path and no feasible fallback exist, and it is defined as granting no authority. Service during an interval in which u_s cannot be established as satisfied contributes u_s(t)=0 to the integral of §3.2; the resulting interval is accounted as an outage against T_{\mathrm{outage},s}. \square

B.5 Soundness and incompleteness of the reference policy

Observation B.5. Let \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{ref}} denote the fail-closed mapping \textsc{unc}\mapsto\textsc{rej} and \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{perm}} the permissive mapping of §5.4.

· \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{ref}} is sound with respect to the certified predicate: every authorization satisfies \mathcal{C}_s^{\mathrm{cert}}. It is incomplete: it may reject actions that would in fact have been compliant, because indeterminate evidence is collapsed to rejection.
· \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{perm}} is more complete but not sound in the same sense: an authorization may rest on an indeterminate constraint, so the coverage guarantee of Lemma B.2 no longer holds for the restricted class without a separate argument.

Consequence for evaluation. The two policies are not two implementations of the same claim; they are two different claims. Reporting them in the same table without labeling which soundness statement applies would be a category error. NCC-Bench v0.1 reports the reference policy as primary and the permissive policy only as a sensitivity analysis, as specified in §5.4.

