Appendix C — Fallback and emergency state machine: formalization

C.1 Transition relation

Let S be the state set of §7.1. A configuration is a pair (q,\xi) where q\in S and \xi is the evidence state (the collection of certified intervals and their ages at the current instant). The transition relation is

(q,\xi)\;\longrightarrow\;(q',\xi')

with labels drawn from \{\text{prop},\text{verify},\text{auth},\text{rej},\text{unc},\text{expire},\text{init},\text{ok},\text{fail},\text{emrg},\text{rec}\}. Each transition carries a guard g_{q\to q'}(\xi) taken from the table of §7.2. A transition is enabled at (q,\xi) iff its guard holds.

C.2 Guard exclusivity

For the relation to be deterministic — which §7 requires, since fallback must be deterministic — the guards leaving each state must be mutually exclusive except where a declared priority resolves the overlap. The declared priorities are:

State Overlap Resolution
\mathrm{VER} \textsc{auth} vs \textsc{rej} vs \textsc{unc} exhaustive and mutually exclusive by Definition 5.1
\mathrm{VER} \to\mathrm{FB} vs \to\mathrm{HOLD} fallback feasibility is the discriminating predicate; exactly one holds
\mathrm{FB} \to\mathrm{REC} vs \to\mathrm{HOLD} \mathrm{HOLD} takes priority: a fallback that cannot satisfy \mathcal{K}_s is not a recovery
\mathrm{TRANS} \to\mathrm{REC} vs \to\mathrm{FB} vs \to\mathrm{HOLD} priority \mathrm{REC}\succ\mathrm{FB}\succ\mathrm{HOLD}
\mathrm{HOLD} \to\mathrm{EMERG} vs \to\mathrm{REC} \mathrm{REC} takes priority: a compliant path obviates the derogation

C.3 Safety invariants

The following invariants are required to hold on every reachable configuration. They are safety properties and are checkable by inspection of the transition table.

Invariant Statement Source
I1 q=\mathrm{AUTH}\Rightarrow \mathcal{C}_s^{\mathrm{cert}}(\xi)=1 at the instant of entry §5.4, Def. 5.1
I2 q=\mathrm{HOLD}\Rightarrow no authorization is in force §7.3, Lemma B.4
I3 q=\mathrm{EMERG}\Rightarrow a derogation record is open and scoped §7.4, §7.5
I4 q=\mathrm{AUTH}\Rightarrow t-t_a\le\tau_{\mathrm{auth}} §5.8, Lemma B.3
I5 q=\mathrm{EMERG}\Rightarrow t-t_e\le\tau_{\mathrm{emerg}} §7.4
I6 Any configuration with no compliant path and no feasible fallback has q\in\{\mathrm{HOLD},\mathrm{EMERG}\} §7.2

I6 is the honesty invariant: the state machine cannot represent an assured configuration when neither assurance nor fallback is available. It is the formal counterpart of the statement in §7.3 that the boundary between assured operation and best-effort degradation is made explicit.

C.4 Liveness is not claimed

NCC v0.3 asserts safety invariants only. It does not assert that the machine eventually returns to \mathrm{MON}, that fallback eventually succeeds, or that \mathrm{EMERG} is eventually exited. All three are conditional on assumptions about path availability that the framework cannot guarantee (§2.1, §8.4). A liveness claim, if one is ever made, must be stated as a conditional claim with its own assumption set and is a task for the Mathematical Model artifact (pending).

C.5 Reproducibility requirement

The guard predicates of §7.2 and the priorities of C.2 must be frozen in NCC-Bench v0.1 before any comparison is run. A state machine whose guard thresholds are tuned after seeing results is not a reproducible object, and the ablation design of §10.1 would not isolate the contribution of the fallback mechanism.

