Appendix D — Certified-interval constructions

The three coverage assumptions of §8.2 are given below as constructions. For each, the appendix states what the interval is, what assumption is required for its coverage statement, and what the failure condition is. In no case is coverage asserted unconditionally.

D.1 Model-based bound

Let \hat\theta(t) be a model-based point estimate with estimated standard error \hat\sigma(t). The interval is

I_\theta(t;\delta)=\bigl[\,\hat\theta(t)-z_{1-\delta/2}\,\hat\sigma(t),\ \ \hat\theta(t)+z_{1-\delta/2}\,\hat\sigma(t)\,\bigr].

Coverage assumption. The model residuals are approximately normal, zero-mean, and homoscedastic over the evaluation horizon; the estimator is consistent.

Failure condition. The model is misspecified, or the residual distribution drifts outside the calibration regime. Because the failure is silent — the interval continues to be produced — this construction is the least defensible of the three for the TN–NTN regime, and is included only because it is the default in much of the engineering literature.

D.2 Age-weighted conformal bound (reference implementation)

Let \{(z_i,\theta_i)\}_{i=1}^{n} be a calibration set of evidence–outcome pairs, ordered by age, with z_n the most recent. Let the nonconformity score be R_i = |\theta_i-\hat\theta(z_i)|, and let the age weight be

w_i \;=\; \lambda^{\,n-i},\qquad \lambda\in(0,1],

where \lambda is the decay parameter. The weighted empirical distribution of scores is

\widehat F_n(r) \;=\; \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\,\mathbb{1}[R_i\le r]}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i}.

The interval is

I_\theta(t;\delta)=\Bigl[\,\hat\theta(z_t)-\widehat F_n^{-1}\!\bigl(1-\delta\bigr),\ \ \hat\theta(z_t)+\widehat F_n^{-1}\!\bigl(1-\delta\bigr)\,\Bigr].

Coverage assumption. Weighted exchangeability of calibration and test scores under the weight sequence w_i. Under this assumption the coverage statement of §5.4 holds with the weighted quantile in place of the unweighted one; the argument is the standard weighted-conformal argument and is not reproduced here.

Failure condition. Weighted exchangeability fails — for example, under abrupt regime change (a beam switch that changes the latency law discontinuously). The construction does not fail silently: as the weights concentrate on recent scores, the effective calibration size shrinks and the interval widens. This is the property that motivates its choice as the reference implementation in §8.2.

Interaction with \tau_{\mathrm{age}}. The decay parameter \lambda and the authorization validity \tau_{\mathrm{auth}} are coupled. If \lambda is set so that weights decay on a timescale shorter than \tau_{\mathrm{auth}}, the interval is calibrated on evidence older than the authorization window and the coverage statement is not meaningful. The freeze sheet of Appendix J records the pair (\lambda,\tau_{\mathrm{auth}}) as a joint parameter.

D.3 Worst-case deterministic bound

Let \Theta(t)\subseteq\mathbb{R} be a declared uncertainty set for \theta at time t, constructed from physical or protocol bounds rather than from data. The interval is

I_\theta(t;\delta)=\bigl[\,\inf\Theta(t),\ \sup\Theta(t)\,\bigr].

Coverage statement. Coverage is 1 by construction over the set \Theta(t). This is not a probabilistic statement; it is a statement about the declared set. Its value depends entirely on whether \Theta(t) is defensible, and its weakness is that a defensible set is often so wide that the interval is indeterminate and the decision is \textsc{unc} by §5.4.

Failure condition. The set is misspecified, or it is unbounded — in which case the interval is indeterminate and the engine must return \textsc{unc}.

D.4 Staleness monotonicity as a requirement on the method

§8.3 requires

\tau_{\mathrm{age}}^{(1)}\le\tau_{\mathrm{age}}^{(2)}\ \Longrightarrow\ I_\theta^{(1)}\subseteq I_\theta^{(2)}.

This is a requirement, not an automatic property. For D.3 it holds by construction if \Theta(\cdot) is chosen so that uncertainty sets grow with age. For D.2 it holds if the weight decay is chosen so that the weighted quantile is non-decreasing in the age of the test point — a condition on (\lambda,\ n) that must be established for the chosen configuration. For D.1 it does not hold in general, which is a further reason to treat D.1 as a fallback rather than a reference.

The proof of staleness monotonicity for the chosen reference configuration is deferred to the Mathematical Model artifact (pending), as stated in §12. The requirement is stated here so that an implementation that violates it can be rejected without waiting for the proof.

