Appendix F — Contract admissibility

Remark 3.1 makes admissibility a design-time obligation. This appendix states the condition and gives one worked instance.

F.1 Condition

A contract \mathcal{K}_s is admissible under assumption set \mathcal{A}_0 if there exists at least one operating state x\in\mathcal{X}(\mathcal{A}_0) such that \mathcal{C}_s(x)=1. Equivalently, the feasible set

\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{K}_s;\mathcal{A}_0)=\{\,x\in\mathcal{X}(\mathcal{A}_0): \mathcal{C}_s(x)=1\,\}

is non-empty.

Admissibility is necessary for the framework to be useful: an inadmissible contract cannot be satisfied by any admissible operating state, and the Assurance Engine will return \textsc{rej} or \textsc{unc} for every proposal regardless of the decision engine's quality. In that case the comparison of §10.1 measures the contract, not the architecture.

F.2 Sufficient condition for the essential-messaging profile

Let the profile require A_{\min}=0.99, T_{\max}=\tau_o, and let the single-path regime have a maximum transition interruption \tau_{\mathrm{int}} with \tau_{\mathrm{int}}<\tau_o. Then a sufficient condition for non-empty feasibility at the profile level is

\frac{\tau_o}{W}\ \ge\ 1-A_{\min},

because the availability loss contributed by an outage of duration \tau_o inside a window W is at most \tau_o/W, and the constraint A_s(W)\ge A_{\min} is satisfiable if the outage budget implied by T_{\max} does not by itself exhaust the availability budget. The condition is sufficient, not necessary.

Worked instance (illustrative, not a measured result). With A_{\min}=0.99, W=60\,\mathrm{s}, T_{\max}=250\,\mathrm{ms}, the ratio \tau_o/W=250/60000\approx 0.00417 is well below 1-A_{\min}=0.01, so the profile-level condition is satisfied with margin. The numbers are chosen to match the tens-to-hundreds-of-milliseconds LEO interruption range of §6 and are illustrative only; they are not a contract recommendation and must be replaced by profile-derived values in NCC-Bench v0.1.

F.3 What admissibility does not establish

Admissibility establishes that the contract is not self-contradictory. It does not establish that the evaluated architecture achieves it. The distinction is the same as the one in §2.4 between a conditional guarantee and measured performance: admissibility is a property of the contract, feasibility is a property of the operating state, and achievement is a property of the evaluated system. Conflating the three would be the precise error the framework is designed to prevent.

