Appendix G — False-permit rate estimation

G.1 Estimator

With k false authorizations among n non-permit cases, the point estimate is \hat p=k/n. Because the quantity of interest is an upper bound on a failure rate, the one-sided 95\% Clopper–Pearson bound is reported:

p_{\mathrm{ub}} \;=\; \mathrm{Beta}^{-1}\!\bigl(0.95;\ k+1,\ n-k\bigr).

For k=0 the zero-event bound is

p_{\mathrm{ub}} \;=\; 1-0.05^{\,1/n}.

G.2 Sample-size consequence

To certify p_{\mathrm{ub}}\le p^\star with zero observed events requires

n \;\ge\; \frac{\ln 0.05}{\ln(1-p^\star)} .

For p^\star=0.10 this gives n\ge 29; for p^\star=0.05, n\ge 59; for p^\star=0.01, n\ge 299. These figures are a planning constraint on NCC-Bench v0.1: an evaluation campaign with fewer than roughly thirty non-permit transition instances cannot report a zero-event bound below 10\%, and any claim of a lower bound from such a campaign would be unsupported.

G.3 Interpretation guard

\mathrm{FPR} is a property of the decision rule and the proposal distribution of the decision engine. Two assurance layers evaluated on different proposal distributions have different FPRs for reasons unrelated to their assurance logic. This is why §10.2 holds D fixed across the AI-only and NCC arms: without that control, the FPR comparison is not attributable to the assurance layer. The figure is reported with the sample size n and the count k alongside it; the bound alone is uninterpretable.

