Appendix I — Falsifiability

The framework is designed to be refutable. The observations below would count against it, and the evaluation design of §10 is structured so that each is observable.

Observation What it would refute
The NCC arm does not reduce hard-constraint violation rate relative to the AI-only arm at matched D The claim that independent assurance improves service continuity in this regime
The NCC arm's FPR is not lower than the AI-only arm's FPR at matched D and matched n The claim that the veto logic of §3.3 correctly rejects infeasible proposals
Removing fallback (ablation AI+A) does not change the hard-constraint violation rate The claim that deterministic fallback contributes to continuity
Removing assurance (ablation AI+F) does not change FPR The claim that the assurance layer contributes beyond fallback
Authorization frequently decays before transition initiation (\tau_{\mathrm{auth}} binding) That \tau_{\mathrm{auth}} can be chosen short enough for staleness control and long enough for practical operation in the same regime
Emergency override frequency is a large fraction of transitions That the framework's assured operating region covers the realistic operating envelope
FPR bounds are reported without adequate n (Appendix G.2) The evidential value of the FPR comparison, independent of the framework's merit

I.1 What would not falsify the framework. A poor measured availability in absolute terms would not, by itself, refute the framework: §2.4 and §2.2 separate measured performance from conditional guarantee, and a regime in which no path is available (Appendix C, invariant I6) is explicitly outside the assured region. The framework is refuted by the comparative results above at matched conditions, not by the absolute difficulty of the regime.

I.2 Asymmetry of the claim. Because v0.3 claims no theorem and no novelty, the only claims available to be falsified are (i) the internal coherence of the contract semantics, (ii) the satisfaction of the invariants of Appendix C, and (iii) the comparative usefulness measured in §10. The first is checked by inspection, the second by the state-machine audit, the third by the evaluation campaign. This is a narrower claim surface than a theorem would provide, and it is stated deliberately.

